{"title": "Components/Charts/Speedometer", "docs": {"text": "Speedometer\n\nWeb\nAndroid\niOS\nFlutter\nCMP\n0\n20\n40\n60\n80\n100\n120\n140\n160\n180\n200\n120 mb/s\nCurrent Speed\nShow code\nCopy code\n\nName\tDescription\tDefault\tControl\nvalue\t\nValue to display on the speedometer\nnumber\n-\t\n\nend*\t\nMaximum value of the speedometer\nnumber\n-\t\n\nstep*\t\nStep value for the ticks\nODSSpeedometerStepProps\n-\t\nEdit step as JSON\nstart*\t\nMinimum value of the speedometer\nnumber\n-\t\n\nscheme\t\nColour scheme for the active arc. Use this to pass a secondary scheme to the line indicator element.\nstring\n-\tSet string\nwidth\t\nWidth of the component.\nnumber\n350\nSet number\nheight\t\nHeight of the component.\nnumber\n320\nSet number\nsvgProps\t\nAdditional props for the line indicator element.\nODSComponentPropsWithRef<\"svg\">\n-\tSet object\nshowMetrics\t\nWhether to show metric labels on the speedometer.\nboolean\n-\tFalseTrue\nspeedometerContentSlot\t\nCustom content to display in the center of the speedometer (e.g., speed value)\nReactNode\n-\tSet object\nshowTopic\t\nWhether to show the current value label on the speedometer.\nboolean\ntrue\nFalseTrue\nGeneral information\n\nThe Data Viz Speedometer is a dynamic component designed to visualise single-value data points within a defined range. It fills up as data arrives and makes changes visible at a glance. Use it when speed, progress, or performance needs to feel immediate \u2013 like in internet speed tests or live metrics.\n\nWhen to use:\n\nShow real-time progress or results (e.g. download or upload speed)\nSurface a single metric that needs to be read at a glance\nUse a gauge when direction and intensity matter more than exact precision\nWhen not to use:\n\nCompare multiple values or datasets (use Data Viz Bar instead)\nShow static progress or loading states\nDisplay values that don\u2019t change or don\u2019t benefit from motion\n\nAnatomy\n\n\nUse Directives\n\nShow Metrics\n\nTrue\n\nShow scale values when users need context for the range or when the position on the scale matters, not just the final value. This is useful for unfamiliar ranges or when users need help interpreting the result.\n\n\nFalse\n\nHide Metrics when the central number is enough to understand the result. This works well in dashboards with multiple Speedometers (e.g. download, upload, latency), where users compare values across metrics rather than against a scale. It reduces visual noise and keeps the layout easy to scan.\n\n\nShow Topic\n\nTrue\n\nShow Content when the exact value is the primary information users need. The center should carry the key number or status so it can be read instantly without relying on the gauge.\n\n\nFalse\n\nHide Content when the value is secondary or already shown elsewhere. This shifts focus to the overall shape or trend of the gauge and reduces duplication in dense layouts.\n\n\nScale and spacing\n\nSteps are evenly spaced along the arc to keep the gauge balanced and easy to read. The values behind those steps are fully configurable, so the scale can match different ranges (e.g. 0\u2013100 vs 0\u20131000) or uneven progressions (e.g. 0, 10, 50, 100).\n\nProduct Handoff\n\nThe specific values used in design templates are for presentation purposes; the final pacing must be defined during the technical handoff to match the live product data.\n\n\nDos and don\u2019ts\n\nProgress Indicator \u2013 on White\n\nThe exposed \u201cColour\u201d property of the .ODS Speedometer Cycle Line Indicator nested instance is allowing the Progress Indicator to appear in Neutral and Secondary schemes.\n\nAll combinations are allowed on White schemes.\n\n\nProgress Indicator \u2013 on Black\n\nAll combinations are allowed on Black schemes.\n\n\nProgress Indicator \u2013 on Magenta\n\nOnly \u201cNeutral\u201d Progress Indicator property is allowed on Magenta scheme.\n\n\nProgress Indicator \u2013 on Secondary schemes\n\nOnly the \u201cNeutral\u201d Progress Indicator property is allowed on a Secondary scheme.\n\n\nBehaviour\n\nShape\n\nDesigned for modern and soft UI presentations, the pilled bar features fully rounded corners. It is ideal for interfaces where a friendly, approachable aesthetic is preferred or when the gauge needs to stand out as a key visual element. Use this variant to add \"visual polish\" in dashboards or marketing-focused data visualizations.\n\nDisclaimer\n\nDue to Figma limitations, the corner radii of the gauge may appear slightly uneven in design files. In development, the component renders with consistent geometry.\n\nData States\n\nProgressive Loading\n\nProgressive Loading \u2013 The gauge fills as data is received, reflecting the current state of the measurement in real time.\n\nFluctuation (calculating state) \u2013 While the value is still being calculated, the gauge subtly fluctuates. The endpoint moves slightly up and down to indicate ongoing activity.\n\nFinal state \u2013 Once the value is confirmed, the gauge settles into its final position and remains stable.\n\n\nHandoff to development\n\nStep Configuration (Metrics)\n\nThe number of steps around the gauge is configurable. Adjust the count to match the needs of the data.\n\nSteps are always evenly spaced to maintain a balanced and readable layout. The values assigned to each step are independent of their position, allowing for non-linear scales (e.g. 0, 10, 50, 100).\n\nValues shown in design are illustrative. Final step values and scaling should be defined during implementation based on real product data.\n\n\nColour Schemes\n\nBehavior on Color Schemes\n\nThe use of this component is restricted \u2013 it can only be used with specific combinations of component settings and schemes.\n\nWhite\n\n \nBlack\n\n \nMagenta (Restricted)\n\nMagenta (Restricted)\n\n\nSecondary (Restricted)\n\nThis scheme is restricted. Only the Neutral Progress Indicator property can be used on Secondary schemes.\n\n\nExamples\n\nWeb\n\n\nMobile\n\n\nAccessibility\n\n", "code": [""], "stories": [{"id": "story--speedometer-docs--example", "html": "<div id=\"story--speedometer-docs--example-inner\" data-name=\"Example\" lang=\"en\"><div><div style=\"padding: 8px;\"><div tabindex=\"0\" start=\"0\" end=\"200\" step=\"20\" value=\"120\" style=\"position: relative; width: 350px; height: 320px;\"><svg width=\"350\" height=\"320\" viewBox=\"0 0 350 320\"><path d=\"M 97.38795969696454 252.6520403030355 A 109.76 109.76 0 1 1 252.61204030303549 252.65204030303548\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--colours-basic-background-subtle, #F1F1F1)\" stroke-width=\"29.119999999999997\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"></path><path d=\"M 97.38795969696454 252.6520403030355 A 109.76 109.76 0 0 1 97.38795969696454 252.6520403030355\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--colours-basic-accent, #E20074)\" stroke-width=\"29.119999999999997\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"></path><circle cx=\"97.38795969696454\" cy=\"252.6520403030355\" r=\"4.48\" class=\"ODSSpeedometerCycleLineIndicator-ArcDot\"></circle><circle cx=\"252.61204030303549\" cy=\"252.65204030303548\" r=\"4.48\" class=\"ODSSpeedometerCycleLineIndicator-ArcDot\"></circle><circle cx=\"97.38795969696454\" cy=\"252.6520403030355\" r=\"4.48\" class=\"ODSSpeedometerCycleLineIndicator-ActiveArcDot\"></circle><circle cx=\"97.38795969696454\" cy=\"252.6520403030355\" r=\"4.48\" class=\"ODSSpeedometerCycleLineIndicator-ActiveArcDot\"></circle><text x=\"68.9905513645128\" y=\"281.04944863548724\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" class=\"ODSSpeedometerCycleLineIndicator-TickLabel\" aria-hidden=\"true\">0</text><text x=\"32.41760707703057\" y=\"221.36782779669215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" class=\"ODSSpeedometerCycleLineIndicator-TickLabel\" aria-hidden=\"true\">20</text><text x=\"26.925763977976942\" y=\"151.58734500116861\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" class=\"ODSSpeedometerCycleLineIndicator-TickLabel\" aria-hidden=\"true\">40</text><text x=\"53.712172203